# The oracle images, pinned by digest.
#
# One line per oracle: <name> <image-ref> <version> <what it answers>
# Tab-separated, machine-read by tools/oracle/oracle_env.py, and checked by
# check-oracle-env - which needs no engine and no reference, so it runs in
# `make test` while the gates that consult an oracle do not.
#
# A stock ref carries a tag *and* a digest. The tag is for a human reading this
# file; the digest is what runs. A tag can be moved under you and a digest
# cannot, so a green run names a set of bytes rather than a set of names.
#
# The version field is checked at run time against what the reference says it
# is, and for both oracles here it names a **UCD version, not the tool's own
# release**. That is the pin that decides what a comparison means.
#
# It is also not a theoretical distinction. CPython 3.13.5 and 3.13.15 both
# report `unicodedata.unidata_version == "15.1.0"` and disagree about
# `unicodedata.decomposition()` for all 11,172 Hangul syllables - so the UCD
# version alone would have pinned two different references. Checking the version
# field is what catches a drifted *table*; the digest is what catches a drifted
# *implementation* of it. Both are needed and they are not the same guarantee.
#
# Raising a pin is deliberate. A new reference version is new answers, and new
# answers that disagree are findings rather than regressions until someone has
# read them.

# The gating pin for NFC: a released interpreter. UCD 16.0.0 against this
# library's 17.0.0, so the skew is not closed here - it is *stated*, which the
# host never could. Measured, over the population the gate generates from the
# pin: the host's 15.1.0 answers 3,412,112 of 4,411,532 sequences and skips
# 999,420; this pin answers 3,476,501 and skips 935,031.
#
# What the skew cannot do is change an answer. Over the 3,412,112 sequences the
# host already answers, UCD 15.1.0, 16.0.0 and 17.0.0 return identical NFC -
# zero differences - so moving off the host adds coverage and determinism and
# corrects nothing. That is the honest case for this conversion and it was
# measured before it was made.
python	docker.io/library/python:3.14-slim@sha256:7bf6c3111fe094f8ee1a1cbcdc63c4cfb345b0e3df42d5aa9a90b3b4b022ab6d	unicodedata 16.0.0	NFC, and which codepoints the reference assigns

# A second CPython, not a replacement. UCD 17.0.0, an exact match with
# tools/idna/UCD_VERSION, which takes the same population to 3,596,802 compared
# and 814,730 skipped - and those 814,730 are structural rather than skew: they
# are codepoints 17.0.0 does not assign either, so no reference reaches them and
# this gate cannot pass 81.5% by any pin.
#
# It is a release candidate, which is why it is not the gating pin: gating on an
# RC is not something to do. It is what `check-nfc-oracle-strict` uses, outside
# TEST_GATES, because a matching UCD is the only configuration in which a
# disagreement is necessarily a defect. When 3.15.0 is released this becomes the
# gating pin and the line above it stays, for the reading between the two.
python-next	docker.io/library/python:3.15-rc-slim@sha256:4c269ae51995d15c7115f3403bde620e5aa5387ca5b865e56a61a4a24c9ffb49	unicodedata 17.0.0	The same question at this library's own UCD version

# `tomllib`, the second TOML reader. Same image as the gating `python` pin, and a
# pin of its own because it answers a different question and the version field
# has to say so: `tomllib` is part of CPython and has no release of its own, so
# what decides its answers is the interpreter and not a UCD. A line saying
# "unicodedata 16.0.0" beside a TOML comparison would name the wrong thing.
#
# It was the host's until now, and that is the whole reason this entry exists.
# The TOML corpus's encode rows are the only place a second implementation
# appears in this library's TOML score, and they were reading whatever `python3`
# this machine happened to have - a reference nobody chose, of a version nothing
# recorded. `check-toml-oracle` uses the same pin over generated documents.
tomllib	docker.io/library/python:3.14-slim@sha256:7bf6c3111fe094f8ee1a1cbcdc63c4cfb345b0e3df42d5aa9a90b3b4b022ab6d	python 3.14	TOML 1.0.0, as CPython's standard library reads it

# toml++ with its unreleased-features set on: the second reader for the
# relaxations TOML v1.1.0 makes, and the only one there is. Nothing else outside
# this repository reads the draft at all.
#
# **Built here, and the pin is the header's SHA-256**, not this tag: toml++ is a
# single header, so its bytes are the implementation and `v3.4.0` is a name for
# them that a maintainer could move. containers/tomlpp/SOURCES carries the hash
# and the Dockerfile refuses the build without it. The driver's own SHA-256 is
# compiled into the driver and checked by the gate, because an image carrying one
# built from an older copy of driver.cpp would answer confidently and wrongly.
#
# **Its set is not v1.1.0's**, and that is the thing to read before trusting a
# clean run. 3.4.0 implements eight unreleased items, cherry-picked from the TOML
# master branch and the issue list. Four of them are v1.1.0 relaxations this
# library implements - omitted seconds in a time, newlines and trailing commas in
# an inline table, `\e`, and `\xHH`. Two are not in v1.1.0 at all: hex floating
# point (#562) and `+` in a bare key (#644). Two are v1.1.0's unquoted-key
# relaxations (#687, #891), which this library does not implement. So the
# comparison runs over the constructs both target and says which it left out;
# tools/oracle/toml_1_1_diff.py is where that list lives, by name, rather than
# here where it could drift from the code that uses it.
tomlpp	localhost/ghoti-text-oracle-tomlpp:3.4.0	toml++ 3.4.0	TOML v1.0.0 plus its own unreleased set, four of which are v1.1.0's

# python-idna, and the one pin here that changes a verdict rather than only
# making it reproducible.
#
# **Built here, not pulled, and the tables are why.** `idna` vendors its own
# generated UCD data, so `idna.idnadata.__version__` is a property of the
# package: no interpreter, container or otherwise, can move it. Debian 13
# packages 3.10, whose tables are 15.1.0, and that gap *is* the 925-codepoint
# blind spot check-idna-oracle used to print - codepoints this library assigns,
# the reference does not, and both call DISALLOWED, so a DISALLOWED of ours that
# should have been PVALID agreed with the reference's ignorance and passed.
#
# 3.19 is not the newest release. It is the one whose tables are 17.0.0, an
# exact match for tools/idna/UCD_VERSION, and the match is the point: at a
# matching version that blind spot has no members. 3.20 carries 18.0.0 and is
# the obvious `idna-next`, deliberately not added yet - a reference *ahead* of
# the pin inverts the skew rather than removing it, and the bucket it needs
# ("the oracle assigns it and we do not") does not exist in the differential.
# That is the next piece of work, not a line in this file.
#
# Being built here, the pin is not a digest - two builds of one Dockerfile are
# not two copies of one image. What is pinned is everything the build reads: the
# base by digest and the wheel by the SHA-256 PyPI publishes for it, with
# --require-hashes so that pip refuses anything else. See containers/idna/.
idna	localhost/ghoti-text-oracle-idna:3.19	idnadata 17.0.0	The IDNA2008 derived property, and UTS #46's mapping table

# The Desktop Entry references, **both of them, in one built-here image**.
#
# This is the only pin whose line names two references, and the reason is that
# they answer different halves of one question and disagree with each other about
# the answer. GLib's `GKeyFile` says what a value *is*: it is the permissive
# reader, and it accepts documents the specification forbids - a duplicate group,
# a duplicate key, a key outside `A-Za-z0-9-`, an orphan `key[de]`, an indented
# line. `desktop-file-validate` says whether a document is *legal*, and refuses
# every one of those. The specification sits with the validator.
#
# So a differential against `GKeyFile` alone would agree with its blind spot and
# score clean while violating §3.2, and one against the validator alone could not
# check a single value. Both, over the same bytes, in one pass. The driver asks
# them together for that reason and not for speed: two passes would not be
# guaranteed to have seen the same document.
#
# **Neither reference answers an unknown escape.** `GKeyFile` parses `k=a\qb` and
# refuses it only at `g_key_file_get_string()`; the validator accepts it outright.
# So those documents have no oracle, and tools/oracle/ini_diff.py excludes them
# **and counts them** rather than scoring them - the count is printed, because an
# exclusion nobody counts is a hole in the denominator.
#
# The version field names both packages plus the driver's own SHA-256, and the
# gate checks the printed line against it. What pins the two references is the
# base digest in containers/inidesktop/Dockerfile together with these versions: a
# Debian point release that moved either package changes the printed line and
# fails the check, rather than silently changing what the comparison means.
inidesktop	localhost/ghoti-text-oracle-inidesktop:2.84.4	glib 2.84.4, desktop-file-utils 0.28-1	Desktop Entry: what a value is, and whether the document is legal

# The git config reference, **built here and one reference rather than two**.
#
# This is the opposite case to the inidesktop line above, and the contrast is the
# useful part. Desktop Entry needs two references because GKeyFile and
# desktop-file-validate answer different halves of one question and disagree; git
# config needs one because the same program answers both halves - it refuses a
# file outright rather than warning about it, so "is this legal" and "what is this
# value" have a single authority and there is no second opinion to be had.
#
# That is a weaker position than the Desktop Entry gate is in, and saying so is
# the point of this paragraph. A differential against a lone reference cannot
# distinguish "we agree" from "we are both wrong the same way", so
# tools/oracle/ini_git_diff.py adds a third reading that does not come from git
# at all: the generator states what each document is *meant* to be, and the
# `intent` score checks our verdict against that. A rule both git and this
# library got wrong would still pass `legality`; it would fail `intent`.
#
# **Built here rather than pulled** because the driver has to frame the batch
# protocol. git is a Debian package, so what pins the reference is the base digest
# plus the version the driver prints, which the gate checks against this line -
# and the driver's own SHA-256 is compiled in, because an image carrying a driver
# built from an older copy of driver.c would answer confidently and wrongly.
#
# The upstream version here, 2.47.3, is the same one this machine's own git is, so
# the probes recorded in notes/text/INI-DIALECTS.md §A.4 and the gate's reference
# are the same implementation. That is luck rather than design and will not
# survive the next Debian release; the pin is what makes it checkable either way.
gitconfig	localhost/ghoti-text-oracle-gitconfig:2.47.3	git 1:2.47.3-0+deb13u1	git config: whether the document is legal, and what every value is

# The EditorConfig references, **two of them in one image, and they disagree** -
# the good case, and the same shape as the inidesktop line above. This is the
# opposite situation to gitconfig's lone reference in every way that matters.
#
# What makes it different from the Desktop Entry pin is that neither reference is
# an authority. EditorConfig is the only one of these formats with a **normative
# conformance suite** - specification 0.17.2 says a conforming core "must pass the
# tests in the core-tests repository" - and `make conformance-ini-editorconfig`
# scores that directly, pinned by tools/conformance/EDITORCONFIG_SUITE_COMMIT.
# **Both cores score 33 of its 34 grammar assertions.** So agreeing with them
# everywhere would be a failure, and tools/oracle/ini_ec_diff.py is built around
# saying so: it scores our verdict against the specification (`intent`), scores
# values only over documents free of a construct a core is known to get wrong, and
# has a third score that asserts each known departure **is still there** - a core
# fixed upstream fails loudly instead of quietly inflating the second.
#
# **Their agreement is worth less than GKeyFile's and desktop-file-validate's.**
# The sharing there is incidental - two programs from one community, different
# code. Here it is structural: both cores descend from Python's `ConfigParser`,
# core-py says so in its own docstring and core-c is `inih`, which cites it too.
# The two places they agree while contradicting the specification - truncating a
# value at a whitespace-preceded `#` or `;` - are exactly that inherited
# behaviour. Where they disagree with *each other*, the specification decides.
#
# **Built here rather than pulled** because Debian's `editorconfig` package folds
# the suite version into its own version string, and what decides these answers is
# core-c's source at a commit and core-py's pip version. The C driver's SHA-256 and
# the Python driver's SHA-256 are both compiled or substituted in and printed, for
# the reason the gitconfig line gives.
editorconfig	localhost/ghoti-text-oracle-editorconfig:0.12.11	editorconfig-core-c 0.12.11, editorconfig-core-py 0.17.1	EditorConfig: what every value is, twice, from two implementations that disagree

# The systemd reference, **one reference and an instrument that answers a different
# question than it appears to**.
#
# `systemd-analyze verify` exits **0** on a syntax error. It warns, skips the
# offending line, and keeps the file; the exit status reports *semantic* failure, such
# as a service with no `ExecStart=`. So this gate reads the **diagnostics** and
# classifies them, and tools/oracle/ini_sd_diff.py holds the list of which strings are
# about the line grammar. A gate built on the exit status would have scored every
# syntactically broken document as legal, and would have printed clean while doing it.
#
# **Values come back through `Environment=`** and nowhere else: no verb prints a
# parsed setting, but that one validates each word as `NAME=VALUE` and reports a
# failure verbatim, after unquoting, unescaping and word splitting. Two limits of that
# channel are measured and handled in the denominator rather than worked around - it
# rewrites a CR to an LF, and truncates a message at 2,097 bytes - because an oracle's
# limits belong in the count.
#
# **This machine has no systemd**: no `systemd-analyze`, no `systemctl`, PID 1 is
# `init`, while 164 unit files sit under /usr/lib/systemd shipped by other packages.
# So the corpus is local and the reference cannot be, which is the reverse of the
# Desktop Entry pin - and the reason this was the last of the five dialects rather
# than the third. Whether this oracle was usable at all was an open question in
# notes/text/INI-DIALECTS.md §A.13 from the first day of the INI work to the day it
# was answered.
#
# systemd and this module **disagree on purpose**, and the gate is shaped around it:
# systemd keeps a file with a bad line and drops the line, while this module refuses
# the document. So the comparison is the *presence* of a grammar fault, not the
# recovery. A reader whose caller cannot see a warning must not silently lose a
# setting.
systemd	localhost/ghoti-text-oracle-systemd:257.13	systemd 257.13-1~deb13u1	systemd unit syntax: whether a line has a grammar fault, and how a value splits into words
